From c10121106dcab130f197439a03846be5075bd74c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 14:45:08 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 66/98] pvr, pco: load 32-bit literals from a shared register
 pool

A literal that is not in the hardware constant table costs a whole
instruction group (bbyp0bm_imm32) to materialize, and a temp to hold it.
For pipelines the driver builds, pco_const_imms() now puts up to 32 of
them in shared registers after the ones the driver allocated, and the
descriptor upload program DMAs their values (a per-pipeline buffer, the
existing PVR_BUFFER_TYPE_COMPILE_TIME path) along with push constants.
Instructions then read the shared register directly.

Shared registers are only valid in hardware sources S0, S2 and S3, so a
literal is pooled only if every use can read it there; otherwise the
legalizer would put a mov before every use. fadd/fmul/fmad uses must be
the first source (p1 reads S3-S5), or a second source that legalization
can swap with a temp. The pool also stops at max_coeffs.

BXM-4-64, interleaved A/B x4 vs the previous build:
  vkheavy 2048: FS 691 -> 680 groups, 11 -> 8 temps, 180.4 -> 171.9 ms
  cstpf: 119 -> 117 groups, ~136 -> ~144.5 M inv/s
  cstpi: 119 -> 115 groups, ~142 -> ~147 M inv/s
Output hashes (cstp/cstpf/cstpi/bits/wg48/wg96/vkheavy) unchanged.

CTS (5062 + 9170 + 838 dot/matrix + 282 buffer cases): identical to before.
